title: c1 Thread in the pocket, person in reach
syntax: xwiki/2.1
%%WIKI-SYNC-META%%
**Abstract.** One pattern: who repairs, who tells, who keeps safe. The speaker gets the first chance to repair, the companion the second, the System the third. Painful truths go to a trusted person. Safety stays with staff, whom the System calls. Consent and settings stay with the resident.

= Team Design Pattern - Thread in the pocket, person in reach [TDP-01] =

**Report Format:** §2.4 Team Design Patterns (Task Level)

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|TDP-01
|Name|Thread in the pocket, person in reach
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|(((
Literature and group reasoning only. Not yet evaluated.

* Derived from SA-01, SA-02, PS-01, PS-02, UC01, UC02
* Order of repair: HFC-03 (Schegloff et al., 1977)
* Partners miss each other's slips: HFC-05 (Bora et al., 2015)
* Painful truths from a trusted person: HFC-08
)))
|Problem|People with dementia lose the thread of a conversation. A partner with dementia often misses it; staff cannot walk every walk. Painful questions go to whoever is near. Who does what?
|Solution Structure|(((
1. Speaker: first chance to repair.
1. Companion (fellow resident): talk, repair, comfort.
1. System: keeps the thread, waits, redirects gently, watches pace, asks consent, defers.
1. Trusted staff: painful truths; physical help.
1. Coordinator: pairs, settings, relationship map.
)))
|Solution Description|The System keeps the thread outside the walkers' heads, and offers it back only after the speaker has had a moment. Anything painful or physical goes to a person: the System calls, it does not tell.
|Per-Actor allocation|(((
**Resident (ST-01, ST-02):**

1. Consent, or not; set own preferences.
1. Talk; repair own slips first.
1. Pause the guide; end the walk.

**Relative (ST-03):**

1. Tell the life story; list welcome and off-limits topics.
1. Agree with staff what is said about painful topics.
1. Consent on the resident's behalf, where needed.

**Care worker (ST-04):**

1. Clip on the guides; check they report ready.
1. Answer alerts.
1. Answer listed topics as agreed with family.

**Activity coordinator (ST-05):**

1. Agree settings with each resident.
1. Pair residents, with both consenting.
1. Keep the relationship map current with ST-04 and family.

**System:**

1. F1: ask first.
1. F2: keep the thread; redirect gently.
1. F3: watch pace and rest.
1. F4: defer listed topics.
1. F5: call for help.
1. F6: six-item record.
)))
|Consequences: benefits|Conversations recover without exposing the slip. Walks with company need less staff time. Painful news comes from the same trusted person each time. Expected effects, still to be tested: UC01-F2-CL1, UC01-F1-CL5, UC02-F4-CL6.
|Consequences: risks|(((
* Companions rely on the guide and stop helping
* The guide cuts in before self-repair (UC01-F2-CL3)
* Help after a slip feels like correction (UC01-F2-CL2)
* An out-of-date map lets a secret through, or misses a sensitive topic
)))
|Related Patterns|IDP-01 Wait, then redirect gently; IDP-02 Ask first; IDP-03 Truthful deferral.
|Connection to Claims|Rationale for UC01-F2-CL1, UC01-F1-CL5, UC02-F4-CL6. Tested against UC01-F2-CL2, UC01-F2-CL3.
|Validation path|EM-01 (the hand-offs work), EM-03 (slips recovered, per case), EM-04 (residents, relatives and staff accept the split).

== Open items to check or research ==

|=Item|=What is needed|=Reason
|Relationship map (O-4)|Form, owner, review after every event.|An out-of-date map is the main route to a disclosure.
|Garden policy (O-6)|Agree with ST-07 and ST-06 who may walk alone or in pairs.|Without it, the allocation has no permission.
|Companion briefing|One page for walking partners and families: what the guide does, and what it never does.|Companions must not assume the guide watches over the resident.

== Graph edges ==

allocates → SA-01, SA-02 tasks to ST-01 to ST-05; F1 to F6 to System; supports → UC01-F2-CL1, UC01-F1-CL5, UC02-F4-CL6; tested-against → UC01-F2-CL2, UC01-F2-CL3.

== References ==

* Bora et al. (2015); Schegloff et al. (1977). See a1.1 References.
